# THE LAYERS WHOSE NAMEPLATE UNIT CARRIES A PERIOD. One row per layer whose amount is a
# RATE -- `4 GPU-hours per week` -- as against a STOCK, `12 people`.
#
# ⭐⭐ WHY THE DISTINCTION IS A RELATION AND NOT AN `IF`. "What fraction of the period is
#    this supply available?" is a malformed question against a stock: `12 people` has no
#    period, so "five days" has nothing to be five days of. Against a rate it is perfectly
#    answerable, and `notApplicable` there is a refusal dressed as a category error. The set
#    of units that HAVE a denominator is therefore the thing that decides whether an absence
#    is honest, and it deserves a name.
#
# ⛔⛔ THE TWO SPELLINGS ARE THE SAME READER'S JUDGEMENT `buffer_term` MAKES, WRITTEN IN SQL
#    INSTEAD OF IN A TABLE. `per` and `por` are one concept under two editions, and deciding
#    they are the same is a judgement no filing makes. It is visible here rather than buried,
#    but it is less honest than layers/absorber.sqlc, where the same decision is a row
#    somebody can delete.
-- pm:Nameplate/amount unit, English or Portuguese edition.
SELECT n.filing, n.layer, n.amount_unit AS unit
FROM pm.nameplate n
WHERE n.amount_unit LIKE '% per %' OR n.amount_unit LIKE '% por %'
